        "Cambridge and Edexcel IGCSE are the most common qualifications taken by fourteen- to sixteen-year-olds at British-curriculum schools across Spain, well ahead of the IB Diploma in raw numbers, because IGCSE is the entry stage nearly every such school runs before splitting students toward IB or A Level in the sixth form. Long-standing British communities on the Costa del Sol, in Valencia and Alicante, and across the Canary and Balearic Islands mean IGCSE has a genuine local track record here, not a recent import.",
        "Structurally, IGCSE is modular: students take individual subjects, typically seven to ten of them, rather than a fixed diploma. Many subjects offer Core and Extended tiers, with Core capping the available grade range and Extended reaching the top grades; the tier decision is usually made a year or more before the exam and is not always easy to reverse. Grading appears as A* to G at most schools in Spain, with 9-to-1 numeric grading used for a smaller number of UK-aligned syllabuses. Examination series run in May/June, with a second October/November series that gives resit options Bachillerato's EBAU calendar has no equivalent of.",
